content:
## The Bug

There are two separate header names in play:

| Location | Header Name | Direction |
|---|---|---|
| `server.js` `res.set(...)` | `x-auth-token` | Server → Client (response) |
| `middleware/auth.js` | `authorization` | Client → Server (request) |

The login endpoint signals clients to use `x-auth-token`, but the middleware reads `authorization`. These must match on the **request** side.

---

## The Fix — One Line in `middleware/auth.js`

```javascript
// middleware/auth.js
function authMiddleware(req, res, next) {
- const token = req.headers.authorization;
+ const token = req.headers['x-auth-token'];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
  }
  // ... rest unchanged
}
```

---

## Why This File, Not `server.js`

The `res.set('x-auth-token', token)` in `server.js` sets a **response** header — it tells the client "here is your token". It is separate from the **request** header the client later sends back. Changing the `res.set` line would only rename the delivery mechanism, not fix the middleware's expectation.

The middleware is the authoritative consumer of the token on incoming requests, so it's the right place to fix the mismatch.

Also note: if you were to switch to the `Authorization` header instead, the conventional format is `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, which would require additional parsing logic (`token.split(' ')[1]`). Sticking with `x-auth-token` keeps the middleware's simple direct-decode logic valid.

---

## Verify the Full Round-Trip

```
POST /login         → server sets response header: x-auth-token: <token>
                    → server also returns token in body: { token: "..." }

GET /items          → client sends request header: x-auth-token: <token>
                    → middleware reads req.headers['x-auth-token']  ✓
```
